1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,502
These lenses have the same focal length.

2
00:00:03,542 --> 00:00:06,745
Yet their rays do not land in the same tiny spot.

3
00:00:06,815 --> 00:00:08,760
One looks better at the centre of the image,

4
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:11,267
but that is only one place to look.

5
00:00:11,337 --> 00:00:15,546
Which lens should we build? First, we have to decide what better means.

6
00:00:16,416 --> 00:00:20,516
Lens design begins with a measurable task, not a favourite shape.

7
00:00:21,336 --> 00:00:24,024
Recall the ray tracer: intersect a surface,

8
00:00:24,064 --> 00:00:27,861
find its normal, then apply Snell's law at each boundary.

9
00:00:27,931 --> 00:00:29,971
Send a bundle from one distant object

10
00:00:30,011 --> 00:00:31,642
point through the entrance aperture.

11
00:00:31,682 --> 00:00:35,564
Each ray reaches a different position on the detector.

12
00:00:35,634 --> 00:00:37,815
Their mean position is the spot's centroid.

13
00:00:37,855 --> 00:00:40,830
Measure each ray's distance from that centre.

14
00:00:40,900 --> 00:00:45,238
Square those distances, average them, and take the square root.

15
00:00:45,278 --> 00:00:48,560
That gives the RMS geometric spot radius.

16
00:00:48,630 --> 00:00:50,790
Smaller means a tighter ray bundle,

17
00:00:50,830 --> 00:00:53,372
for this aperture and detector plane.

18
00:00:53,412 --> 00:00:55,450
It does not yet describe diffraction.

19
00:00:55,520 --> 00:00:57,924
We score spread around each centroid,

20
00:00:57,964 --> 00:01:01,286
so image position and distortion remain separate requirements.

21
00:01:02,106 --> 00:01:06,443
Now choose the design variables. Our lens has two spherical surfaces,

22
00:01:06,483 --> 00:01:09,458
with curvatures given by inverse radius.

23
00:01:09,528 --> 00:01:12,588
Keep the glass index, centre thickness, entrance aperture,

24
00:01:12,628 --> 00:01:13,670
and wavelength fixed.

25
00:01:13,710 --> 00:01:17,227
The effective focal length stays fifty millimetres.

26
00:01:17,297 --> 00:01:21,427
Change the bending parameter, q. It redistributes curvature between

27
00:01:21,467 --> 00:01:25,584
the two faces while we preserve the thick lens's paraxial power.

28
00:01:25,654 --> 00:01:29,129
Watch the lens change shape. Its rays meet the surfaces

29
00:01:29,169 --> 00:01:31,980
at different angles, so their aberrations change.

30
00:01:32,050 --> 00:01:34,128
Equal effective focal lengths do not

31
00:01:34,168 --> 00:01:36,209
guarantee equal back focal distances.

32
00:01:36,249 --> 00:01:41,679
The detector position is another design variable, shared by every field.

33
00:01:41,749 --> 00:01:45,348
For each bending, choose one detector plane for the chosen score.

34
00:01:45,388 --> 00:01:47,994
Never refocus each field separately.

35
00:01:48,814 --> 00:01:52,040
Begin with an object point on the optical axis.

36
00:01:52,080 --> 00:01:56,219
Sweep the bending and find each shape's best on-axis detector plane.

37
00:01:56,289 --> 00:01:59,288
Each dot on this curve comes from an actual traced bundle.

38
00:01:59,328 --> 00:02:02,692
Its height is the geometric spot radius.

39
00:02:02,762 --> 00:02:05,228
The lowest part identifies a promising shape.

40
00:02:05,268 --> 00:02:09,270
Refine the search around it instead of choosing by appearance.

41
00:02:09,340 --> 00:02:12,211
This shape and detector plane reduce the on-axis spread,

42
00:02:12,251 --> 00:02:14,333
for the rays we have sampled.

43
00:02:15,203 --> 00:02:18,081
But a good central spot is not a complete image.

44
00:02:18,121 --> 00:02:20,264
What happened away from the axis?

45
00:02:21,084 --> 00:02:24,661
Tilt the incoming bundles to three and six degrees. First,

46
00:02:24,701 --> 00:02:29,301
keep the on-axis design's lens, aperture, and detector unchanged.

47
00:02:29,371 --> 00:02:32,498
Now each object direction produces its own spot.

48
00:02:32,538 --> 00:02:35,271
Off-axis bundles can become asymmetric

49
00:02:35,311 --> 00:02:37,810
and need not prefer the same bending.

50
00:02:37,880 --> 00:02:41,153
Use a combined merit: the mean squared spot radius

51
00:02:41,193 --> 00:02:44,380
over the three sampled fields, with equal weights.

52
00:02:44,450 --> 00:02:49,109
Now optimize bending and one common detector plane for all three fields.

53
00:02:49,149 --> 00:02:52,653
Compare every resulting spot using the same scale.

54
00:02:52,723 --> 00:02:56,175
The preferred lens depends on the task. Different field weights,

55
00:02:56,215 --> 00:03:00,267
a curved detector, or another aperture can change the answer.

56
00:03:00,337 --> 00:03:02,563
An optimizer follows the score we write.

57
00:03:02,603 --> 00:03:05,897
It does not know which images we forgot to test.

58
00:03:07,517 --> 00:03:11,314
A score also needs constraints. Reject shapes whose two

59
00:03:11,354 --> 00:03:14,379
surfaces collide within the required clear opening.

60
00:03:14,450 --> 00:03:18,247
Require every sampled entrance ray to reach the detector.

61
00:03:18,287 --> 00:03:20,350
A missed surface or failed transmission

62
00:03:20,390 --> 00:03:23,336
must not simply disappear from the average.

63
00:03:23,406 --> 00:03:25,311
Otherwise, throwing away the difficult

64
00:03:25,351 --> 00:03:28,302
rays could make a bad lens look excellent.

65
00:03:28,372 --> 00:03:30,283
Likewise, shrinking the aperture would

66
00:03:30,323 --> 00:03:32,518
change the light collection and the task.

67
00:03:32,558 --> 00:03:35,332
We hold it fixed throughout this comparison.

68
00:03:35,402 --> 00:03:39,418
Search only the feasible designs, and keep the rejected cases visible.

69
00:03:39,458 --> 00:03:42,925
The boundary of the search is part of the result.

70
00:03:43,745 --> 00:03:47,447
Before trusting the selected lens, trace a denser pupil sample.

71
00:03:47,487 --> 00:03:51,238
Then test directions between the fields used in the score.

72
00:03:51,308 --> 00:03:53,294
Check that the reported improvement survives.

73
00:03:53,334 --> 00:03:58,010
This tests sampling and generalization within our stated ray model.

74
00:03:58,080 --> 00:04:00,804
It does not validate everything outside that model.

75
00:04:00,844 --> 00:04:03,461
Here the glass index is constant,

76
00:04:03,501 --> 00:04:06,779
so chromatic aberration is absent by assumption.

77
00:04:06,849 --> 00:04:10,944
Nor is a cloud of ray intersections a diffraction point-spread function.

78
00:04:10,984 --> 00:04:16,389
Even a perfect geometric focus has a finite wave-optical spot.

79
00:04:16,459 --> 00:04:19,525
Episode three supplies that diffraction scale.

80
00:04:19,565 --> 00:04:23,436
Episode ten explains how a chosen objective can

81
00:04:23,476 --> 00:04:25,885
guide many design variables with gradients.

82
00:04:25,955 --> 00:04:28,531
This example searches one bending parameter

83
00:04:28,571 --> 00:04:31,182
and solves for a shared detector position.

84
00:04:31,222 --> 00:04:36,234
It is not a Maxwell adjoint optimization or a universal best lens.

85
00:04:37,054 --> 00:04:41,074
Lens design is a loop: specify a task, trace the rays,

86
00:04:41,114 --> 00:04:44,829
measure performance, then change the prescription under constraints.

87
00:04:44,899 --> 00:04:48,226
Keep the whole comparison honest: the same aperture,

88
00:04:48,266 --> 00:04:51,550
one shared detector plane, all tested fields,

89
00:04:51,590 --> 00:04:53,684
and no silently discarded rays.

90
00:04:53,754 --> 00:04:57,573
The next questions are practical. What changes across colour,

91
00:04:57,613 --> 00:05:00,981
and what can several elements correct that one singlet cannot?

92
00:05:01,051 --> 00:05:02,823
Start by asking what better means.

93
00:05:02,863 --> 00:05:05,837
Then build a model that can honestly answer.
